    <section class="cv-block" id="legit" aria-labelledby="legit-title">
      <h2 id="legit-title">Are Udemy Linux Foundation exam vouchers legitimate?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes — with the same distinction as every other voucher on this site. Udemy is the{" "}
        <strong>seller of the voucher</strong>, not the certification body. The Linux Foundation owns the certification
        and PSI delivers and proctors the exam. That is why the code is redeemed in the Linux Foundation My Portal, not
        inside Udemy.
      </p>
      <ul class="cv-check">
        <li><strong>Who sells it:</strong> Udemy, on udemy.com. The receipt and code come from Udemy.</li>
        <li><strong>Who delivers the exam:</strong> PSI, through the Linux Foundation portal. Online and remotely proctored only.</li>
        <li><strong>Who issues the certification:</strong> the Linux Foundation / CNCF. Udemy cannot issue, renew or verify it.</li>
        <li><strong>How to verify your code:</strong> it appears in My learning → Certifications and is accepted when you redeem it in the Linux Foundation portal.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="cv-note">
        Red flags: a seller asking for bank transfer or crypto, codes sent by direct message, or prices far below the
        Linux Foundation price. Vouchers are non-transferable, so a code bought from a stranger may not work. Buy from
        the official Udemy voucher pages or the Linux Foundation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="cv-block" id="worth-it" aria-labelledby="worth-title">
      <h2 id="worth-title">Are Udemy Linux Foundation exam vouchers worth it?</h2>
      <p>
        It depends on whether the 30% survives the regional price difference — and whether you value the simulator. At
        US$445 list, a real 30% saving is about US$133.50, which is substantial. But if Udemy's local price is higher, or
        you have to buy the simulator separately, the gap narrows quickly.
      </p>
      <h3>When the Udemy voucher makes sense</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>The live voucher price in your country is clearly below the Linux Foundation price after currency conversion.</li>
        <li>You are already buying a prep course on Udemy and want one checkout.</li>
        <li>You will redeem the code within 6 months and sit the exam within the following 12 months.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>When buying direct is better</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You want the Killer.sh simulator bundled without having to confirm what Udemy includes.</li>
        <li>The Udemy price matches or exceeds US$445 in your region.</li>
        <li>You want a single support path for registration, scheduling and retakes.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Either way, remember</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>All three exams are performance-based and hands-on — a voucher does not replace lab practice.</li>
        <li>Certifications last 2 years; factor recertification into the total cost.</li>
        <li>CKS requires CKA first, so do not buy CKS expecting to skip the prerequisite.</li>
      </ul>
